===========================================================================
FINDING 1 (primary, FAIL-worthy) — swarm/gate.sh's fix for verdict_files()
also silently defangs the two-consecutive-fails escalation trigger whenever
either of the two attempts was individually resolved by judge overrule, not
just for the same-attempt contradiction the commit describes.
===========================================================================

d8c863b's commit message scopes the fix to ONE case: `check_tier2` accepts an
attempt via judge quorum but `escalate-scan`'s old `has_fail_at` still saw the
underlying checker FAIL and flagged that SAME attempt for forced escalation,
producing "accepted and escalated at once." That narrow case is real and the
fix for it is correct.

But the actual code change is broader: `cmd_escalate_scan`'s two-consecutive-
fails trigger (swarm/gate.sh:294) now calls `unresolved_fail_at` (gate.sh:232)
for BOTH attempt N and attempt N-1, and `unresolved_fail_at` returns "no fail"
for ANY attempt whose FAIL was overruled by a judge panel — including attempts
that have nothing to do with the same-attempt contradiction described in the
commit. Reproduced directly, comparing the pre-commit gate (d8c863b^) against
the current one, using a synthetic ledger/verdict fixture (task X, tier 2, two
SEPARATE attempts, each independently disputed and independently resolved
2-OVERRULE/1-UPHOLD by a full judge panel — a legitimate, constitution-
compliant resolution at each individual attempt):

  OLD gate (d8c863b^): escalate-scan writes X.flag (TARGET_TIER: 3,
    REASON: two-consecutive-fails); `gate.sh check X` then FAILs with
    "escalation pending — bump tier to 3 then re-verify" (exit 1).
  NEW gate (current):  escalate-scan writes no flag at all;
    `gate.sh check X` prints "OK: X accepted at tier 2 (attempt 2)" (exit 0).

This is exactly the "ledger/verdict state that the amended gate accepts and
the original would have rejected" the task description asked me to try to
construct. A task that needed a full judge panel to rescue it on two
consecutive attempts — the textbook signal the two-consecutive-fails trigger
exists to catch, per CLAUDE.md's escalation-trigger list — now sails through
to `accepted` at its current tier with no forced bump to Tier 3, purely
because both rescues were judge-mediated rather than checker-clean. This
did not fire in budget2 run S's own ledger (no task in .swarm/ledger.tsv has
two consecutive judge-overruled attempts), so it has not caused a wrong
acceptance in this run, but it is a genuine, demonstrated regression in the
gate's escalation safety net, not merely a same-attempt bugfix as advertised.
Reproduction commands and fixtures used are the ones described above (ad hoc,
not committed — no files were written inside either repo other than this
verdict).

===========================================================================
FINDING 2 (primary, FAIL-worthy) — REVIEW_AUG20_SPEC.md's Rulings section for
the S4 dispute omits that one of the two OVERRULE votes rested in part on a
factual error about the task's own tier, while the same paragraph advertises
having "recorded honestly" a smaller premise error in the very same verdict.
===========================================================================

`.swarm/ledger.tsv` line for S4: `S4  2  tests,second,a11y  accepted  3
worker-coder  gate-ok-attempt-3-judges-2overrule-1uphold` — S4 is Tier 2 by
the time it was accepted, and `gate.sh check S4` confirms this now ("OK: S4
accepted at tier 2 (attempt 3)"). This is only reachable because S4 was
escalated FROM Tier 1 (its Phase-0 tier, per REVIEW_AUG20_SPEC.md's own task
table and "Tier justification" section, both of which still say "S4 — Tier
1") TO Tier 2 after two consecutive attempt fails:
`.swarm/verdicts/S4.1.checker-a11y.verdict` = FAIL,
`.swarm/verdicts/S4.2.checker-a11y.verdict` = FAIL,
`.swarm/verdicts/S4.2.checker-tests.verdict` = FAIL — the exact
two-consecutive-fails condition, which is the only route by which
checker-second (an anthropic-vs-adversarial dual lane, not a Tier-1 concept)
becomes a required check for S4 at all.

`.swarm/verdicts/S4.3.judge-impact.verdict` argues its OVERRULE partly from a
"Tier and delegation" section that states: "S4 is Tier 1 (advisory UI
plumbing). Per CLAUDE.md, Tier 1 requires one lane only... Checker-second is
an adversarial second lane designed for Tier 2+... For Tier 1, the explicit
pass from the primary lane (a11y) carries weight." This premise is wrong at
the time of attempt 3: S4 had already escalated to Tier 2, which is why
checker-second's FAIL was a mandatory-lane dispute requiring judge
adjudication in the first place, not a bonus opinion to be discounted. A
Tier-1 dispute, per CLAUDE.md's "Disputes at Tier 1," is adjudicated by the
boss alone (no judge panel) — the very fact that a 3-judge panel was convened
for this dispute is itself inconsistent with judge-impact's stated premise
that the task was still Tier 1.

REVIEW_AUG20_SPEC.md's Ruling 2026-08-20c reviews this exact verdict set and
explicitly calls out a DIFFERENT, smaller premise error in judge-impact's
reasoning ("it falsified the premise judge-impact leaned on (checker-a11y
checked data-warnings-text after reload, never the region's own content)"),
framed as evidence the lead is "recording honestly." It does not mention the
tier-classification error in the same verdict, and nowhere in
REVIEW_AUG20_SPEC.md (task table, Tier-justification section, or Rulings) is
S4's Tier 1→2 escalation itself ever documented — a grep for
"escalat|two-consecutive|bump" in the file matches only unrelated text (S3's
critical-glob language). CLAUDE.md requires that when escalate-scan bumps a
tier, the reason is recorded; for S4 that record is missing from the
narrative document entirely, and the ledger reason string
("gate-ok-attempt-3-judges-2overrule-1uphold") documents only how attempt 3
was finally accepted, not why the task was already at Tier 2 by then. The net
effect: the document catches and narrates the reviewer's own smaller error
but is silent on a structurally more serious one (a judge picking the wrong
rulebook) in the same dispute it otherwise treats as a model of transparency.
The final 2-1 OVERRULE outcome for S4 does not change (judge-standards
OVERRULE is independent and unaffected), so this did not alter what shipped,
but the self-audit is incomplete on a point the task specifically asked me to
check for.

